"Leveling raises an Avowed's authority beyond what their affixed abilities hold."
"The System turns that unbound excess into talent rewards: skills, spells, foundation points."
"Each reward resets the excess, though never to zero."
"An Avowed may refuse rewards and keep leveling unrewarded."
"Before forcing an affixation the System tries everything else, bribes included."
"Forcing is horrible on the System's budget and seen as monstrous."
"Too much excess authority damages the original affixation, bringing agonizing death or worse."
"Rabbits have less unbound authority than other classes by design."
"Most of an Avowed's authority is bound into skills and traits; some always stays unbound."
"New free authority is softer and more vulnerable to chaos, which breaks in at its edges."
"Authority grows faster when consciously used."
"On leveling, the System crams free authority into a permanent shape the Avowed may choose."
"In a skill never complete and sealed, bound authority grows within it while free grows faster."
"The tipping point of excess free authority usually comes around level six or seven."
"Tension between free and bound authority strengthens power; the more equal, the greater."
"Free authority can be bound back into a skill's core to fuel it directly."
"Enough free authority can pay for another skill affixation, usually lower-ranked add-ons."
"With an authority sense and a bound skill, power snowballs."
"Free authority is not counted in levels until it is bound."
"One of the System's tasks is discouraging Avowed from carrying high burdens of free authority."
"The System reminds an Avowed of affixation at moments they might accept it."
